## KEYWORDS
Docetaxel; in vitro release; MTT assay; NanoMIL-100
## Introduction
There are vast numbers of drug nanocarriers that can be categorized as purely inorganic nanomaterials (e.g. iron oxide nanoparticles [1], gold nanoparticles [2], zeolites [3]) and purely organic nanomaterials (e.g. polymeric nanoparticles, micelles [4], dendrimers [5] and liposomes [6]). According to recent research, there is a third category, which are called hybrid nanomaterials, such as metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [7]. In 1999, Yaghi et al. introduced these new classes of hybrid materials, which are self-assemblies of metal ions as coordination centres and organic ligands as linkers between metal centres [8]. This kind of porous material has taken advantage of both organic and inorganic compounds. This ordered material with high surface area has substantial properties to adsorb guest molecules like gases or drugs [9].
In the past decade, various types of MOFs with diverse applications, such as gas storage [10], catalyst [11], sensors [12] and membranes [13], have been designed and synthesized. Among them, MILs (Material Institut Lavoisier) were the promising nanocarriers due to their brilliant characteristics in drug delivery applications. Horcajada et al. introduced several porous iron(III) carboxylate nanoparticles; MIL-89, 88 A, 100, 101 and 53 as novel nanocarriers with entrapment capabilities for a variety number of drugs. The different drug release trends from the various MOFs can be the consequences of different types of entities (e.g. hydrophilic, hydrophobic and amphiphilic), which lead to diverse drug–carrier bonds with different strengths [14]. Serre et al. studied encapsulation and release profiles of ibuprofen delivery by MIL-100 and 101 based on chromium materials in comparison with MCM-41 as a pure inorganic nanocarrier. According to the above study, the drug content in MIL-101 was four times larger than that in MCM-41 and it showed a complete drug release in 6 days. They proposed that this behaviour was because of anion–cation electrostatic interactions and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking between the aromatic rings of ibuprofen and the organic part of the framework [15]. Chalati et al. compared busulfan loadings in different MILs such as $88\mathsf{A},$ 53, 89 and 100. Their research showed higher drug loading in MIL-100, which was explained by the larger pore size of this carrier ${\mathord{\left/{\vphantom{\left({\sim25}}\right.\kern-\nulldelimiterspace}}}$ and $29\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ). Among the various MILs investigated in their study, MIL-100 and MIL53 showed sustained release in phosphate buffer saline (PBS) during the first $24\mathsf{h}$ [16]. Moreover, they estimated the amount of ibuprofen loading in MIL-53 (Cr and Fe) as two flexible nanoporous metal-organic frameworks and investigated their in vitro drug deliveries. They revealed that the flexibilities of these MOFs were due to strong host–guest interactions between MOFs as carriers and drug molecules, which resulted in sustained release during 20 days [17].
There has been various research on MIL-100(Fe) as an effective nanocarrier due to its unique characteristics such as biocompatibility [18], stability in biological medium [19], large pore size and amphiphilic entities to adsorb either hydrophilic or hydrophobic drugs [20]. For instance, azidothymidine triphosphate (AZT-TP) was investigated as a highly hydrophilic drug with 24 wt $\%$ loading and $86w t\%$ of drug release after $24\mathsf{h}$ in PBS, $37^{\circ}C$ [21]. Singco et al. considered two approaches for delivering aspirin; the first one applied MIL100 as a carrier which had only $35\%$ aspirin release after $268\mathsf{h}$ in PBS $(\mathsf{p H}=7.4)$ and the second one was a synthesized bioMIL-100 which consisted of aspirin as a second linker beside trimesic acid in MIL-100 skeleton [22]. RAPTA-C is a ruthenium, Ru(II)-based metallodrug with antitumor activity that was released from MIL-100 up to $30\%$ after 3 days [23]. In another research, phosphated gemcitabine (Gem-MP) as a highly hydrophilic prodrug was loaded in MIL-100 about $30\mathsf{w t\%}$ and the synthesized nanoMOF was degraded during $24\mathsf{h}$ release process in PBS [24].
Docetaxel (DTX) is one of the most effective chemotherapeutic agents among all existing anticancer drugs. This taxane is used against a wide range of tumours such as prostate, non-small-cell lung, head and neck, colon and advanced breast cancers [25–27]. Despite its significant clinical efficacy, this drug is associated with off-target systemic toxicity like hypersensitivity reactions, bone marrow suppression, alopecia, cardiac disorders and tiredness that restricts the dose and duration of therapy [28]. Moreover, due to the poor water solubility of DTX, cosolvents and surfactants, such as ethanol and polysorbate80, are required to prepare the injectable formulation, and unfortunately, this formulation causes hypersensitivity reaction and shows incompatibility with common polyvinyl chloride intravenous administration sets [29].
According to well-known phenomena in tumoural tissues, enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect [30], nanoparticle vehicles with and without targeting agent can be effective solutions to address the cytotoxic drug drawbacks and efficiently deliver the drugs to the tumour cells. Hence, in the recent years, various nanocarriers including hydrogels [31], nanoliposomes [32], activated carbon nanoparticles [33], graphene oxide [34], polymer matrix of nanoparticles using mPEG-PLA-PLL copolymer [35], poly(D,L-lactide-co-glycolide)/ hyaluronic acid block copolymer [36] and have been designed and applied for cancer treatments.
In this context, we propose biocompatible MIL-100(Fe) as a novel nanocarrier for delivering DTX to the breast cancer cells. To reach this purpose, MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized by hydrothermal synthesis approach followed by preparation of nanoMIL-100(Fe) using ultrasound technique. Loading process was examined in varied conditions including diverse solvent systems and proportion of drug to nanocarrier (DTX/ nanoMIL-100(Fe)). Controlled delivery of DTX was assayed in PBS medium and finally in vitro release was checked on breast cancer cell-line, MCF-7.
As shown in Scheme 1, at the first step, MIL-100(Fe) was synthesized by hydrothermal process in a Teflon-lined autoclave. MIL-100(Fe) crystals with the large pores have grown from Fe(III) trimer to hybrid supertetrahedra and finally threedimensional microcrystals during 6 days [37]. At the second step, nanoMIL-100(Fe) was prepared using ultrasound method to enhance the accessibility of porosities and consequently to facilitate DTX loading.
## Materials and methods
## Materials
All chemicals in this work were purchased from commercial vendors and were applied without further purification. $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (Merck), 1,3,5-Benzene carboxylic acid or trimesic acid (1,3,5-BTC, Merck), hydroflouric acid (HF, Chem-Lab), nitric acid $(\mathsf{H N O}_{3},$ Merck), dichloromethane (DCM), docetaxel (DTX, Sanofi), polysorbate 80 (Merck), hydrochloric acid (HCl, Merck), dimethyl sulphoxide (DMSO, Merck) and HPLC-grade methanol and acetonitrile were provided from DaeJung. Also, foetal bovine serum (FBS), 3–(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)- 2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT), penicillin, $0.25\%$ trypsin-ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA) and Dulbecco's modified eagle medium (DMEM) cell culture medium were all purchased from Sigma–Aldrich (St. Louis, MO). MCF-7 human breast adenocarcinoma cells were obtained from national cell bank of Iran (NCBI).
## Instrumentation
Sonication process was performed by Bandeline, UW3200, probe-ultrasonicator, Germany. The quantitative determination of DTX concentration was accomplished by high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) system (Knauer,
Germany) equipped with S-2500 UV KnauerSmartline detector. The size and zeta-potential of nanoMIL-100 particles was measured by Malvern Nano-ZS, Zetasizer, UK. The morphology of nanoparticles was investigated by field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) using HITACHI S-4160 apparatus. The FT-IR spectra were recorded using Bruker, Equinox 55 spectrometer equipped with an attenuated total reflectance (ATR) accessory over the range of $600{-}4000{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . The X-ray powder diffraction was performed by Philips, PW1730 with 2h range of $2-20^{\circ}$ , with $0.02^{^{\circ}}$ step width and wavelength of Cu $K_{\alpha}{=}1.5409\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ . The Belsorp Mini 2 (Bel, Japan) was employed to investigate nitrogen adsorption isotherms of MIL-100 which was synthesized by hydrothermal method and prepared nanoMIL-100 as an effective nanocarrier (activated at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ 12 h) and finally DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100 (activated at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ 12 h) at $77\mathsf{K}.$
## Synthesis of MIL-100(Fe)
As previously reported [37], synthesis of MIL-100(Fe) was performed under hydrothermal conditions. A mixture of $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (1 mmol), $H_{3}B T C$ (0.66 mmol), HF (2 mmol), $H N O_{3}$ $(1.2\mathsf{m m o l})$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ $(5\mathsf{m}|)$ was placed in a Teflon-lined autoclave. It was held at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 6 days with heating ramp of $12\mathsf{h}$ and cooling ramp of $24\mathsf{h}$ . In purification step, the orange solid was stirred in hot water for $3h$ and then, it was filtered and washed with water and dried at room temperature.
## Preparation of nanoMIL-100(Fe)
In biological system, there are exclusive properties of nanosized MOF rather than bulk MOF materials. Recently, increased uptake of biomolecules due to enhanced surface areas, effective distribution of the nanoparticles throughout the biosystem and consequently extended blood circulation time has been considered [38]. Regarding to these mentioned features and EPR effect [30,39], we can improve the performance of MOFs as drug vehicles by making nanosized ones. In the past decades, many efforts have been made towards the synthesis of nanoMOF by two approaches: bottom up and top down. Microwave-assisted [40], microemulsion [41] and interfacial syntheses [42] are examples of the bottom-up approach. In this work, we prepared nanoMIL-100 (Fe) by ultrasound irradiation [43,44] which is one of the most prevalent top-down techniques [45].
Therefore, $_{0.02\mathfrak{g}}$ of the synthesized MIL-100 (Fe) was dispersed in $20\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and the flask was fixed in ice bath for controlling flask temperature. Ultrasonic irradiation was carried out at the power of 50 W for 30 min. This process was replicated eight times and the TT13 probe was used in all repetitions. The resulting solid was freeze-dried and activated overnight at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum.
## DTX encapsulation in nanoMIL-100(Fe)
We examined various conditions such as diverse concentrations of nanoMIL-100(Fe):DTX and different solvents. Acetonitrile and dichloromethane were the selected solvents due to the high solubility of DTX in them. For the impregnation process, $0.5\mathsf{m g}$ of previously dehydrated nanoMIL100(Fe) was suspended in $2\mathsf{m L}$ of dichloromethane and 1 mg of DTX was added as well. The prepared suspension was then sealed and stirred for $24\mathsf{h}$ at room temperature. After all, the DTX-loaded nanoparticles were collected by centrifugation (14000 rpm, 15 min, room temperature) and evacuated for 3 days to eliminate any residual solvent. The clear supernatant including unloaded drug in dichloromethane was evaporated and DTX crystals re-dissolved in acetonitrile (1 mL). DTX concentration in acetonitrile was then quantified by HPLC in triplicate, see Table 1.
Additionally, DTX-loaded nanoparticles were characterized by PXRD, FT-IR, ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption and DLS to determine the changes after the encapsulation process.
## HPLC assay for DTX
DTX concentration in the loading and release process was quantified by HPLC equipped with a reverse phase C18 column (MZ-analytical column, PerfectSil Target ODS-3, $250\times4.6\mathsf{m m}$ , pore size $5\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . The mobile phase was acetonitrile/water (65:35) with the flow rate of 1 mL/min and UV detection wavelength was set at $229\mathsf{n m}$ . The injection volume for drug analysis was $50\upmu\up L$ and the run time was 12 min for each sample. The calibration curve was linear by analysing a series of concentrations of DTX in acetonitrile in the range of $0.093{-}1000\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ for loading and release amounts with a correlation coefficient of $R^{2}=0.999$ . The limit of detection (LOD) and limit of quantification (LOQ) were found to be 0.011 and $0.046\upmu{\mathfrak{g}}/{\mathfrak{m L}},$ respectively. Standard samples were prepared and injected in triplicates on three successive days [46].
## In vitro DTX delivery
DTX release from the prepared nanoparticles was studied through suspending $0.5\mathsf{m g}$ of drug-loaded nanoparticles in $4\mathsf{m L}$ of PBS solution $\mathrm{(p}\mathsf{{H}}\ 7.4\$ and 5.5) with polysorbate 80 $(1\%\ v/\ v)$ as a solubilizer of hydrophobic DTX. The suspension was kept under rotative agitation in different incubation times ( $30\mathrm{min}$ to 7 days). At each time interval, the release medium was recovered by centrifugation (18,000 rpm, 10 min) and replaced with $4\mathsf{m l}$ of fresh medium. The concentration of the released DTX at each time was quantified by HPLC. The experiments were performed in triplicates.
## Cell culture
MCF-7 as a human breast cancer cell line was used for in vitro cytotoxicity assay. DMEM supplemented with $10\%$ FBS and $5\%$ penicillin–streptomycin was utilized as the cell culture medium. Before the experiment, cells were cultivated in humidified environment at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ with $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to reach desirable confluence.
## MTT assay
The cytotoxicity of DTX, nanoMIL-100(Fe) and DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) against MCF-7 cells was investigated by MTT colorimetric assay. For this measurement, the cells were seeded into 96-well plates about 5000 cells per well. At first, the cells were incubated for $24\mathsf{h}$ . Then, cell treatment was performed at the drug concentration range of 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 50 and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . According to previous reports on cellular studies of DTX, the cells were incubated for 24, 48 and $72\mathsf{h}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $5\%\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . After each time interval, the old medium was replaced with $50\upmu\up L$ of MTT:PBS solution $(0.5\mathrm{mg/mL})$ . The plates were incubated for $2-4h$ at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and $5\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Afterward, DMSO was added to the wells in order to dissolve the water-insoluble Formazan crystals. Eventually, the absorbance of the solution was quantified by microplate reader (BioTEK ELX800) at the wavelengths of 570 and $630\mathsf{n m}$ as reference.
MTT assay results as the percentage of cell viability were expressed as the mean $\pm mathsf{S D}$ from four individual experiments. The half maximal inhibitory concentrations $(\mathsf{I C}_{50})$ with employing the one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) test were obtained by SigmaPlot software.
## Result and discussion
## Characterization of prepared nanoMIL-100(Fe)
As shown in Figure S1, the hydrodynamic diameter of nanoparticles with narrow polydispersity index (PDI) was about $165\mathsf{n m}$ , $P_{ Ḋ }\mathsf{ Ḋ }}|=0.145$ . However, the FE-SEM images (Figure 1) indicate the probable particle aggregation in the working media [14] that can be related to several factors such as probe power and consequently local temperature, duration of sonication, type of vessel and amount of dispersant [45,47].
According to Figure S2, zeta potentials of hydrothermally synthesized MIL-100(Fe) and nanoMIL-100(Fe) are $-9.68\pm6\nobreakspace$ . $33\mathsf{m V}$ (left) and $-17.6\pm6.32\mathrm{mV}$ (right), respectively. Zetapotential values of nanoMIL-100(Fe) are shifted to more negative values which can probably be due to the existance of more free –COOH groups rather than bonded ones as a result of ultrasound irradiation.
According to Figure 2, the peaks at 1724 and $1630{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ correspond to $\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O}$ bands and the peaks around 1430 and $1570\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are assigned to the stretching vibrations of O-C-O group [15,20]. Also, $v(A r C-H)$ as aromatic bending vibrations are observed at 760 and $709\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and the broad absorption near $3500{-}3000{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ corresponds to O-H stretching vibrations of clathrated water molecules, which are almost eliminated after activation [48] (nanoMIL-100 active, red). By comparing both FT-IR spectra of MIL-100(Fe) and nanoMIL100(Fe), the intensity of $\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{O}$ band of free carboxylic acid at $1724\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ has been increased which may suggest partial degradation of the structure after irradiation.
Due to indexing the pattern, nanoMIL-100(Fe) has the same crystalline structure as synthesized MIL-100(Fe) [16]. As seen in Figure 3, reducing size of the crystals causes the Bragg peaks to broaden and confirms that nanoMIL-100(Fe) possess lower crystallinity compared to the microcrystals that were synthesized by hydrothermal method [37,49].
According to the literature, microcrystals of MIL-100(Fe) has two diverse sizes of pores, which are 25 and $29\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ with windows of 5.5 and $8.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ respectively [37]. Due to the mentioned small sizes of windows in comparison with the large sizes of DTX molecules $(10.5\times12.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ , we prepared nanosized MIL-100(Fe) not only to better pass through the bio barriers but also to increase uptake of DTX molecules on/into MIL100(Fe) nanoparticles.
## DTX loading
The loading process was examined in two different solvents including acetonitrile (samples a and b, Table 1) and dichloromethane (sample c, Table 1) with different ratios of nanoMIL100:DTX. According to the experiments, the considerable drug loading was not achieved in acetonitrile, but by replacing dichloromethane as impregnation medium, a successful DTX loading, ${36.4}\mathrm{wt\%},$ in nano-MIL-100(Fe) was obtained. Also, DTX payload and encapsulation efficiency were measured $57.2\mathsf{w t\%}$ and $28.6\mathsf{w t\%},$ respectively. It should be noted that this amount of loading is not only comparable with small hydrophobic drugs such as ibuprofen $(33\mathrm{wt\%})$ or large ones such as doxorubicin $(9.1\ \mathsf{w t}\%)$ [14] but is also similar to the hydrophilic drug loadings that have been reported so far [14,50]. This result is probably because of the proper hydrophobic interactions of nanoMIL-100 with aromatic rings of DTX which is called $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking [15]. This kind of interaction is highly expectable between molecules with broad conjugation system like ${\mathsf{D T X}},$ as it was observed between DTX molecules and graphene oxide sheets [34]. Regarding to Table 1, the mentioned interaction effectively occurred in dichloromethane. This can be explained by coordination ability of $C H_{3}C N$ that inhibits the interactions between DTX molecule and nanoMIL-100 particles [51].
Additionally, DTX-loaded nanoparticles were characterized by PXRD, FT-IR, DLS and ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption to determine the changes after the encapsulation process.
Regarding to the previous reports, two types of calculations are reported to indicate the percentage of the loaded drug at the first one, drug payload (DP) was calculated as the percentage of entrapped drug to the weight of pristine nanoMIL-100 while at the second one, drug loading (DL) was calculated as the percentage of entrapped drug to the weight of dried particles including loaded drug and nanoMIL100 [21,24] (Table 1). Encapsulation efficacy was accounted as the percentage of entrapped drug to the total amounts of solids [52].
## Characterization of DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100
Comparing the size of nanoparticles before loading $\begin{array}{r}{(198\mathsf{n m},}\end{array}$ , $\mathsf{P D I}=0.301$ , Figure S3(a)) and after loading $(220\mathsf{n m}$ , $\mathsf{P D}\mathsf{I}=0$ . 238, Figure S3(b)) showed no significant particle size differences that may indicate that no significant agglomeration had happened during loading process.
TEM images of MIL-100(Fe)(a) and nanoMIL-100(Fe)(b) confirmed the size reduction of nanoMIL-100(Fe) after sonication process. Moreover, DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe)(c) has the same faceted-type structure as nanoMIL-100(Fe)(b) after encapsulation of DTX, see Figure 4 [53].
Figure 5 displays the FT-IR spectra of DTX (blue), nanoMIL100 (red) and DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100 (green).
The characteristic absorption band of DTX (blue) is the stretching vibration of N-H at 3476 and $3425c m^{-1}$ and outplane vibration of this band was observed at $715c m^{-1}$ [54].
The vibrational bands of nanoMIL-100(Fe) around 1705, 1624, 1447 and $1378(c m^{-1}$ were all observed in the FTIR spectrum of the DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe), which confirmed the stability of the nanoMIL-100(Fe) after loading process without any chemical reaction with DTX molecule. Also, DTX loading in nanoMIL-100 is confirmed by the appearance of N-H out-plane vibration at $707\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $C-N$ stretching vibration of DTX at $1261(m^{-1}$ . The shift of aromatic bending vibrations of C-H from 760 and $709\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ to 802 and $757(m^{-1}$ , respectively, might be due to the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking between aromatic rings of DTX and linkers in nanoMIL100(Fe).
The PXRD patterns of DTX (blue), nanoMIL-100(Fe) (red) and DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) (green) have been shown in Figure 6. The pattern of DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) demonstrates the maintenance of nanoMIL-100 crystallinity after loading process.
MIL-100(Fe) and nanoMIL-100(Fe) were preliminarily dehydrated under vacuum for $24\mathsf{h}$ at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) was vacuumed at $80^{\circ}C$ to avoid degradation of the drug. According to Figure 7 and Table 2, although longer time sonication [55] has led to smaller particles, this also subsequently decreased the crystallinity (Figure 6) and reduced the surface area and the volumetric capacity of nanosized MIL-100(Fe). Therefore, the measured surface area in nanoMIL-100(Fe) decreased rather than MIL100(Fe) crystals, which were synthesized by hydrothermal process. Such a decrease in the surface area was seen in nanoMOFs, which were synthesized by other methods such as spray drying [49]. After loading of DTX, almost no residual porosity was observed. Table 2 shows the surface areas and pore volumes of the structures.
## In vitro DTX release
The release rate of DTX was investigated in two neutral (7.4) and acidic (5.5) pH of PBS including polysorbate $80(1\%\ \vee/\vee)$ . Regarding to Figure 8, in neutral condition after $96\mathsf{h},$ , only $5.08\%$ of entrapped DTX was released, while in acidic condition, DTX release was reached $15.53\%$ . DTX has a sustained and slow release rate either in neutral pH or in acidic one. This slow pace of DTX release was observed in other reported nanocarriers such as carbon nanotubes [56], graphene oxide [34] and different polymeric systems [36,52] which is mainly because of hydrophobic nature of DTX [34].
By altering the pH, the drug release profile just changed in the first phase known as burst release, which was higher in acidic condition rather than neutral one. The higher release rate in acidic condition can be described by $\zeta$ potential. According to Figure S4, the $\zeta$ potential of DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) found $-38.2\pm7.98\mathrm{mV}$ which is higher than nanoMIL-100(Fe) $(-17.6\pm6.32\mathrm{mV})$ ). That can be explained by the adsorption of DTX molecules with a large number of functional groups such as –OH. The protonation of DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) particles in acidic condition may lead to their discharge and subsequently can cause higher DTX release in acidic media as it has been observed in other nanocarriers [27,34,57,58]. The elevation in the burst release under acidic condition can be valuable due to the acidic microenvironment of tumour cells which may offer more efficient cytotoxic delivery to the tumour compared with the healthy cells [59]. Moreover, due to the strong $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking between linkers and aromatic rings of DTX molecule, they will have incomplete release in PBS media.
## Cytotoxicity assay by MTT analysis
The cellular toxicities of free DTX and DTX-loaded nanoMIL100(Fe) were investigated on MCF-7 cell line at the DTX concentration range of 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 50 and $100\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ The drug-free nanoMIL-100(Fe) was also considered to investigate the probable cytotoxicity of the blank carriers on the cells in the same concentration range of the drug-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe). The non-treated cells served as control. As depicted in Figure 9, nanoMIL-100(Fe) shows no cytotoxic effect on the cells, which demonstrates that nanoMIL-100(Fe) can be an appropriate nanocarrier in the examined concentrations for delivering anti-breast cancer drugs as previously reported [60]. It only revealed $94\%$ cell viability in the high concentration $(100\upmu9/\mathrm{mL})$ which has also been observed in other cell lines such as human leukemia (CCRF- CEM), human multiple myeloma (RPMI-8226), human macrophages (J774), human phytohemagglutinin (PHA)-P-activated peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMC), pancreatic cancer cells (PANC-1), lung (A549, Calu-3) and hepatic cells (HepG2, Hep3B) [14,16,21,24].
The ${\mathsf{I C}}_{50}$ of free DTX and DTX-loaded nanoparticles was measured by SigmaPlot software at various incubation times 24, 48 and $72\mathsf{h}$ which were 4.9908, 0.9612, 0.3816 and 0.198, 0.3816, $0.198\upmu{\sf g/m L}_{\protect}$ , respectively (Table 3). After $24-\mathsf{h}$ incubation, MTT was added to the wells containing free DTX and DTX-loaded nanoparticles. DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) showed $67\%$ and $45\%$ cell viability in 0.01 and $0.1\ |\mathrm{\upg/mL}$ concentrations of DTX, respectively. In contrast, the $1C_{50}$ range of free DTX at this time was between 1 and $10\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ with cell viability of $54\%$ and $46\%$ , respectively. One-way ANOVA statistical analysis confirms that DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) at $24\mathsf{h}$ has more significant toxic impact $(p<.05$ in all examined concentrations) on tumour cells than free DTX at all incubation times. According to this test, three groups were compared to each other which are mentioned with different markers in Figure 9(a). It should be noted that the differences observed in control versus DTX and control versus NP.DTX groups were statistically significant $(p<.001)$ at all concentrations and all incubation times.
Due to the results, although the cytotoxicity of DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) on MCF-7 cells was increased with the raise of concentration, there were no significant cell viability differences between the incubation times, 24, 48 and $72\mathsf{h}$ in the cells treated with DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) at each examined concentration (Figure 9(b)).
In cell culture medium, DTX-loaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) particles were surrounded by excessive amounts of biological molecules, such as phospholipids, proteins, DNA and small metabolites, which may result in the saturation of DTX-loaded nanoparticle surface and reduce the strength of $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between aromatic rings of DTX and nanoMIL-100(Fe) and subsequently caused to improve the release profile [61]. Moreover, lysosomal enzymes with the acidic pH may considerably increase the drug release in cytoplasm that finally resulted in comparable cell death rather than free drugs [61].
## Conclusions
Herein, MIL-100(Fe) was hydrothermally synthesized followed by preparing nanoMIL-100 using ultrasound method in order to increase the uptake of docetaxel. DTX as a highly hydrophobic anticancer drug with notable side effects was studied. The loading process was performed in two different solvents such as acetonitrile and dicloromethane. We successfully achieved $57.2\mathsf{w t\%}$ DTX payload and $36.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ drug loading in dichloromethane as impregnation media. In vitro DTX release was carried out in PBS pH 7.4 and ${\mathsf{p H}}~5.5,$ , $1\%(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ polysorbate 80, $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ which was a sustained and pH-dependent release trend. The cytotoxicities of nanoMIL-100(Fe) and DTXloaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) were investigated against MCF-7, human breast cancer cell line. In the same condition, DTXloaded nanoMIL-100(Fe) showed a better inhibitory effect than free DTX on MCF-7 cells. Altogether, regarding to the outcomes, nanoMIL-100(Fe) has indicated promising features which can be introduced as a novel anticancer drug delivery system.
